today I want to talk to you about how  connecting as a whole with the German  language  the very first language I learned myself  made all the difference  at the ripe age of 15 I had been  learning English French and Latin at  school and I told myself it was about  time to learn another language German  which my family constantly talked about  and that's what I did I just got a were  a dusty grammar book that I found at my  grandmother's place and I started to  leave through it and then going through  the exercises yes exactly  exercises that book was full of grammar  that was focused on grammar and that  reminded me of how I learned led and  that was a mistake that I was not aware  of because I was approaching German  intellectually just intellectually with  my mind what you have to do is not only  to connect with the language  intellectually which is great but also  with your mind with your body you have  to connect it emotionally that makes all  the difference because in the long run  you will need other things not just your  mind to keep walking down the language  path  [Music]  so after a few weeks of painfully  leafing through the book and trying to  move forward I told myself this is never  going to happen I'm not going to learn  German but then a lucky turn of events  happened and I actually ended up buying  another course which was more modern  more colorful more engaging and overall  it was really cool I started to use this  this book and it made all the difference  within a few weeks I actually realized  that there were other ways of  approaching language learning material  and I found out about my bi-directional  translation course so I started using  translation to understand and figure out  the basic patterns of a language that  gave me an enormous boost I started  connecting not only with the pages but  also with the language and with myself  and the combination of these three  things really made a huge difference in  the short as well as in the long run so  after finding this cool course and after  finding a method to worked wonder for me  I started making time for the language  when I woke up the very first thing that  came to my mind was not cookies or  breakfast it was German and  automatically I started to plan and make  the time to learn it every single day  even if I went to school even if I was  very busy that made a huge difference  and what I did is that every time that I  completed a task I spent one hour with  German I always made sure that I had a  logbook with me where I put all the  entries every single day looking back  every month or every two months  I actually saw how much ground I had  covered and that gave me extra  motivation to move forward  moving from a merely intellectual to an  intellectual and emotional connection  with the language was huge progress but  one thing was still missing and that was  the human factor so it turned out that  two years into learning German I met a  native German speaker when I was in a  summer camp in Sardinia and the cool  thing about this is that years after  meeting for the first time I established  a very profound relationship a bond with  her and we started exchanging letters  and we did this for years and the  connection I developed with her with the  letters themselves it really made a huge  difference in me wanting to pursue  German for life not just for two or  three years I was not aware of that at  the time but that encounter those  exchanges those words those letters were  all an emotional or a word per se and  those were words really make a  difference in you walking down the  language path and getting to your  destination but there are many other  rewards not just emotional rewards their  intellectual rewards for example the  first time did you understand the scene  of a movie after learning the language  for a little bit the first time that you  go to the shop around the corner and you  get to have a strike a conversation and  a meaningful one of that with a person  and then you have what I call  existential rewards and in short  experiences for example you travel  abroad you start tasting seeing smelling  but not just you go into the country but  also having native speakers coming to  you you can host  native speakers you can have dinner with  them you can go around the city these  are all form of rewards that keep you  going and they will keep you motivated  in down the language path  the bottom line of it all is that you  have to see language-learning  like a holistic organic live experience  it is an intellectual challenge but it's  also an emotional journey and ultimately  it's a set of life-changing experiences  so I got two questions for you the first  one is have you ever tried to learn a  language but given up after a few weeks  can you tell me why that happened and  the second thing is what do you think  are the most important rules of thumb  for you to stay on track and to stay  motivated I'm very eager to know your  opinion in the comment section below so  try to write as much as you can if you  want to know more about how to stay on  track please click on the description  box below the video and you will read a  ton of other additional information  [Music]
 

